<v SPEAKER_03>Campbell thank you for requesting this I think counselor Hirsch requested this report this evening I'm gonna provide some information regarding a possible earned income tax exemption for lower income residents and we have put together a few considerations for you as well as some some data to show you I'm pausing until he gets the presentation up here so before we start I would like to add just a small caveat we've done quite a bit of research on this I hope you'll see that through some of these considerations however I do want to say we haven't taken a thorough dive into any of the particular considerations so the presentation tonight is estimates from what we've learned these estimates will not be exact and they will require further work depending on how council decides to pursue next steps we'll move into further considerations for the options based on on your on your proposals we tried to balance staff time for the purpose of the initial report knowing that this is the first step in the process next slide so tonight's agenda it's a short section so this isn't going to be a long report but there's five different short sections we'll give a quick overview of earned income tax review some of the data from that we'll bring some considerations forward for you to think about as you're making decisions on how to move forward we'll look at some financial estimates we'll have some potential alternatives in here as well and then we'll talk about next steps next slide please so before we dive into the exemption we thought we would give a review of what the earned income tax actually is we've been talking about this quite a bit during the home rule process so the earned income tax or EIT you'll see it referred to as EIT up here is a tax paid on income earned by anyone who lives in the city so the current rate paid by city residents is 1.1 percent of their income generally the state maximum percentage is 0.6 percent but the home rule charter allows municipalities to go higher than that and so the 1.1 was voted on with the budget as of January 1st because of the Home Rule Charter this is the city's portion of the earned income tax the school district also collects an earned income tax and they currently collect 0.5% so the total earned income tax that the city residents pay is 1.6% earned income tax is only paid on income earned so it's not paid on Social Security income so that means that many retired individuals would not be paying the earned income tax next slide please all right so an earned income tax exemption this can be determined for income earners making less than a certain amount the state sets the maximum income earned for the exception at $12,000 and we believe that this is the ceiling regardless of home status for any municipality in Pennsylvania we currently don't have an exemption for the earned income tax and this is what we're going to explore a little bit this evening I will just note there's another tax the local service tax this is collected from anyone who works in the city regardless of where they live we do offer an exemption for that tax the if the income is under twelve thousand and again that's the state maximum so we want to give a few data points for you from across the state so from what we found there's currently we think 36 municipalities in Pennsylvania that are currently offering an earned income tax exemption this is actually 1.4 percent of all municipalities in Pennsylvania so there's about 2,600 municipalities across the state this is according to the DCED website and we discovered that this number might actually still be a little high one of the municipalities shown to be giving an earned income tax exemption is in Lancaster County so we actually reached out to them and they did they were not aware that they were offering this exemption so so that but this is what the DCD list is showing at this time so of the 36 municipalities 11 of them have a population over 10,000 and there's one Ben Salem which is close to Philadelphia that's slightly larger than Lancaster City with a population just over 60,000 all of the other municipalities are smaller than Lancaster so we would be one of the largest to have this exemption about two-thirds of the municipalities have a population under 10,000 the smallest municipality offering this is made up of 422 people so it's quite small and none of the 36 municipalities have an exemption that's above 12,000 they actually range anywhere from 200 up to 12 000 which was interesting to see all right next slide please so some considerations to think about as we were reviewing this our solicitor Mr. Hanwerger reviewed some of the exemption level requirements and as I mentioned we were conferring with some outside consultants as well and they believe that we cannot go above that twelve thousand dollar income level regardless of the home rule status as I stated earlier so I'm focusing data tonight on what it it would look like for Lancaster to do this at the twelve thousand dollar level additionally we met with the Lancaster County Tax Collection Bureau and they actually provided quite a bit of data for us to look into which was really helpful for this process so they also went out and talked to a second tax collection agent agency that works with the large portion of the 36 municipalities who are currently doing the exemptions and these are some considerations that come from both the Lancaster County Tax Collection Bureau as well as this other Tax Collection Bureau one of them is that we would want to ensure that there's some provision that this would only be eligible for anyone who has lived in the city for the full calendar year otherwise someone who has a higher income might move into the city very late in the year but still receive the exemption because of that income threshold the exemption must work on a refund basis this is required for two reasons one is we're not able to determine who is making less than the threshold until after taxes are filed so residents would still need to pay the tax throughout the year and then be refunded after they file the tax the second is actually more important because the school district receives a portion of the tax and that portion is not exempt they would still need to pay that portion so the tax is collected at the full percentage and then divided between the city and the school district so it can't be withheld throughout the year residents would need to file the taxes by the tax deadline in order to have the Tax Collection Bureau have capacity to process the refunds and I I'm going to mention a bit later that the Tax Collection Bureau would be the ones processing these these refunds the the Bureau estimates that only about 50% of residents actually file taxes timely at this point that said if and if there is an exemption as an option we would anticipate that more taxpayers would be filing in a more timely manner and then finally in discussion with the Tax Collection Bureau we've also determined that really the only reasonable option to have these exceptions process would be to have them do the do the processing for us as we talked about it because of some of the confidential taxpayer information it'd be very difficult to get the information needed to city staff or other issues or other entities to actually issue the refunds but they have agreed that they could do the work for a fee so that was that was a helpful step to determine all right next slide please okay so some of the numbers this is where it may get a little complicated so I'm gonna try to keep this as try to explain it as best I can so these numbers are estimates using 2024 numbers we had to make some educated assumptions as I mentioned earlier we don't have historic trends to show us you know this type of exemptions shift at this point so we're using some some estimations here based on the 2024 percentage of tax filers the city would receive approximately 130,000 in income from about 3,600 taxpayers making 12,000 or annually this is if if taxpayers were filing at the at the rate that they are typically filing now we're estimating that approximately 20% more taxpayers would file with the incentive of a refund so that would equal about two hundred fifty eight thousand with a total of about forty six hundred individual taxpayers so the dollar amount as you can see almost doubles from the hundred and thirty thousand to the two hundred fifty eight thousand with this additional filing and that's because the the taxpayers earning closer to twelve thousand would likely be the ones to file at a greater number or this is what we're anticipating so we would anticipate that there would be higher amounts coming in for that and therefore for the purposes of this estimate we're using that two hundred fifty eight thousand we're considering that two 258,000 to be kind of the lost revenue for the city I'm putting that in quotes but that would be technically what would no longer be available as tax revenue so this is where it may get a little complicated but of this 258,000 we now need to consider a non-resident earned income tax the non-resident earned income tax applies to anyone living in the city but working in a different municipality so residents are charged one earned income tax I mentioned that it's together the city and the school district this earned income tax will first go to the municipality in which they live if the municipality that the resident lives in decides not to charge an earned income tax or if it's refunded the municipality that they're working in has a right to collect a non-resident tax so we we actually collect a non-resident earned income tax from this as well and I'll give you just it's it's very a very small amount in June our total earned income tax that came in was five hundred and twenty six thousand non-resident of that the non-resident portion was five thousand so it's about one percent so back to this example if someone lives in the city but works in Mannheim Township Mannheim Township would generally not collect the non-resident tax because the city gets the tax first before the outside municipality would however if the resident living in Lancaster City gets an exemption to that 1.1 percent rate in essence this means that the resident has not yet paid an earned income tax so then Mannheim Township would be eligible to receive their non-resident tax at their rate which at this point is 0.6 percent because they don't have that exemption they would receive that funding and then the resident would receive a refund of 0.5 instead of the full 1.1% so based on what the Tax Collection Bureau could obtain in the last couple of weeks looking at you know residents living in the city and working outside it looks like approximately 117,000 of the 258,000 would be paid to other municipalities the taxpayer would then receive about 141,000 as a refund and the average refund would be about $42 per resident and then additionally we would we would be paying a fee to the Tax Collection Bureau for the work it would be about ten dollars per filing so about forty seven thousand dollars we're figuring so the city's total outlay with the revenue reduction and the expenses for processing would be approximately three hundred and four thousand and a hundred and forty one thousand of that so about forty six percent less than half of the total outlay would actually go back to the taxpayers that's a lot of numbers so the next slide is gonna just graph it out a little bit for us this is showing on the left-hand side what would go to the other municipalities the processing fee and then the refund to residents and so you can kind of see regardless of what what level the numbers are you can kind of see the percentages they're a little bit better as well all right next slide I want to give some potential alternatives to this ultimately council needs to decide about pursuing an exemption however given that potentially more than half of the total financial outlay may not actually end up going back to the taxpayers we wanted to offer some other options so one to consider would be an application process this wouldn't necessarily eliminate the the the pieces that are not going to the taxpayers but it could lower the processing because there might not be quite as many applications that would be coming through this piece we haven't researched fully yet again so that we would need some further consideration if we would want to go that route we could also consider allocating you know basically the same portion that would have been refunded or would have been our total outlay to a program that we might already be doing that that could be helping low-income individuals so a couple of them are listed here the critical repair fund we've talked about that often at council that's that specifically supports low-income individuals in the city and it would mean that all of the funds would actually be kept to be used for city residents we could also consider allocating some to the Lancaster Water Assistance Program that's the program that is offered through CAP for utility support for lower-income households and we could designate this is for all of the households that the program is set up for all of the households that we serve with our utilities but we could designate this portion just for in-city residents and then we could explore new programs if you would like to help lower income households we could research next steps on those as well last slide so ultimately we'll need direction from council on what you'd like to have more information on as I mentioned these are all estimates at this point so once we have a general direction we can work on more concrete information to provide and once the focus or direction is determined we will account for those costs in the budget you know we're currently working on the budget we'll have a final draft hopefully completed by the last week in September so we'd want to at least include some estimates

